> the model converged but gave me this error on CFA:

It is a warning, not an error, so it only means there might be a problem.

>   lavaan WARNING: covariance matrix of latent variables
>                 is not positive definite;
>                 use lavInspect(fit, "cov.lv <http://cov.lv>") to investigate.

A non-positive definite (NPD) matrix could mean there are linear dependencies (redundancies) or it could signal a Heywood case (out-of-bounds value, such as a negative variance or a correlation that exceeds +/-1).  I don't see any negative variances, so try looking at the standardized version:

inspect(fit.cfa.model, "cor.lv <http://cov.lv/>")

If any correlations that exceed +/-1, that's the cause of the warning.  If not, the problem is linear dependency, which is harder to describe/address because it is about a combination of covariances/correlations that would not actually be possible to observe.  But these are estimates about latent variables, and an NPD matrix can happen sometimes due to sampling error (gross model misspecification could also cause an NPD matrix).

> The problem came as I fit regressions using SEM after the CFA model:
> ...
> + effect~HIGH
> + effect~MED
> + effect~LOW

These predictors of effect sound like dummy codes representing 3 categories of a single variable.  And the category labels sound exhaustive.  Is there a fourth group beyond high, medium, and low?  You only need to include k - 1 dummy codes to represent k groups, because the last group is implied by all the other dummy codes equaling zero.  Including a dummy for every group could be causing your convergence problem.

Terrence D. Jorgensen
Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics
Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam
http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen

